WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.278 --> 00:00:03.433
An accessible service also needs a usable way to

2
00:00:03.456 --> 00:00:06.900
say that something is not working. Standard fourteen addresses

3
00:00:06.922 --> 00:00:11.211
culturally and linguistically appropriate ways to identify, prevent and

4
00:00:11.233 --> 00:00:16.167
resolve conflicts, complaints or grievances. Standard fifteen addresses communication

5
00:00:16.189 --> 00:00:19.367
about progress in implementing and sustaining CLAS.

6
00:00:19.978 --> 00:00:23.711
For an individual learner, the practical starting point is

7
00:00:23.733 --> 00:00:27.622
knowing the appropriate route and explaining it clearly. You

8
00:00:27.644 --> 00:00:31.444
do not need to personally investigate every concern. You

9
00:00:31.467 --> 00:00:33.844
do need to avoid leaving someone without a next

10
00:00:33.867 --> 00:00:36.956
step simply because the issue belongs to another part

11
00:00:36.978 --> 00:00:38.022
of the organization.

12
00:00:39.449 --> 00:00:42.404
Imagine someone says that they could not understand the

13
00:00:42.427 --> 00:00:45.316
service-planning notice and missed the opportunity to ask for

14
00:00:45.338 --> 00:00:49.516
assistance. An unhelpful response would focus only on proving

15
00:00:49.538 --> 00:00:52.582
that the notice was sent. A more useful response

16
00:00:52.604 --> 00:00:56.049
acknowledges the concern, clarifies what the person needs now,

17
00:00:56.204 --> 00:00:58.049
and follows the applicable process.

18
00:00:58.780 --> 00:01:01.802
Do not promise a particular outcome, a deadline you

19
00:01:01.826 --> 00:01:05.824
cannot control, or confidentiality beyond what the process permits.

20
00:01:06.514 --> 00:01:08.891
Explain what you can do, what will happen next

21
00:01:08.980 --> 00:01:11.759
and who is responsible. If there are immediate safety

22
00:01:11.781 --> 00:01:15.492
issues, legal deadlines or urgent service needs, use the

23
00:01:15.513 --> 00:01:18.469
relevant current procedure rather than waiting for a general

24
00:01:18.491 --> 00:01:19.424
improvement review.

25
00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:24.213
A concern may involve a misunderstanding, a service failure,

26
00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:28.591
discrimination, an accessibility issue or another matter requiring a

27
00:01:28.613 --> 00:01:31.259
specific route. Do not relabel it simply to make

28
00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:34.192
it fit the process most familiar to you. Obtain

29
00:01:34.213 --> 00:01:37.637
the appropriate guidance and preserve the person's opportunity to

30
00:01:37.659 --> 00:01:38.792
use available channels.

31
00:01:39.869 --> 00:01:43.802
Ask whether someone can find the concern process, understand

32
00:01:43.824 --> 00:01:46.180
it and use it in a suitable language and

33
00:01:46.202 --> 00:01:49.847
format. A complaint form that is available only through

34
00:01:49.869 --> 00:01:53.647
an inaccessible website can prevent the organization from hearing

35
00:01:53.669 --> 00:01:55.869
the very problems it needs to address.

36
00:01:56.530 --> 00:01:59.419
Consider whether the person is told how receipt will

37
00:01:59.441 --> 00:02:02.686
be acknowledged, what information is needed, and how to

38
00:02:02.708 --> 00:02:06.774
ask about progress. Use approved records and channels. Do

39
00:02:06.797 --> 00:02:09.863
not place complaint details or case information in this

40
00:02:09.886 --> 00:02:11.197
course's reflection notes.

41
00:02:11.822 --> 00:02:15.689
Staff should also know how recurring concerns inform improvement.

42
00:02:16.133 --> 00:02:19.156
Resolving one issue may be necessary without addressing the

43
00:02:19.178 --> 00:02:22.311
condition that caused it. If several people encounter the

44
00:02:22.333 --> 00:02:25.644
same unclear instruction, the organization may need to review

45
00:02:25.667 --> 00:02:28.222
the instruction as well as respond to each person.

46
00:02:29.389 --> 00:02:34.656
Standard fifteen asks organizations to communicate progress. Useful communication

47
00:02:34.678 --> 00:02:39.767
is understandable, accessible and honest about limitations. It explains

48
00:02:39.789 --> 00:02:42.344
what was done, what was learned and what will

49
00:02:42.367 --> 00:02:42.967
happen next.

50
00:02:43.578 --> 00:02:46.667
For our notice, a progress update might explain that

51
00:02:46.689 --> 00:02:50.133
the wording and assistance information were revised after review,

52
00:02:50.533 --> 00:02:53.867
describe how usability is being checked, and identify the

53
00:02:53.889 --> 00:02:56.578
next review point. It should not claim that all

54
00:02:56.600 --> 00:03:00.467
communication barriers have been eliminated because one document changed.

55
00:03:01.131 --> 00:03:04.464
Separate an action from a result. Updating a notice

56
00:03:04.487 --> 00:03:07.331
is an action. Evidence that people can use it

57
00:03:07.353 --> 00:03:10.531
more effectively is a result that requires checking. Keeping

58
00:03:10.553 --> 00:03:13.976
that distinction visible helps avoid overstating success.

59
00:03:15.053 --> 00:03:18.453
Share only information approved for the audience and purpose.

60
00:03:18.898 --> 00:03:21.409
Avoid details that could identify a person who raised

61
00:03:21.431 --> 00:03:24.942
a concern. Follow the appropriate review process for public

62
00:03:24.964 --> 00:03:29.342
communication or organizational statements. This lesson does not authorize

63
00:03:29.364 --> 00:03:31.653
a learner to publish on behalf of the division.

64
00:03:32.353 --> 00:03:35.642
If the evidence is incomplete, say what is known

65
00:03:35.664 --> 00:03:38.953
and what remains uncertain. If a change did not

66
00:03:38.976 --> 00:03:42.398
help as expected, explain the next step rather than

67
00:03:42.420 --> 00:03:45.976
hiding the result. Clear limits are more useful than

68
00:03:45.998 --> 00:03:48.292
a broad assurance that everything is resolved.

69
00:03:49.237 --> 00:03:52.103
In the activity, select a response to an access

70
00:03:52.126 --> 00:03:56.014
concern. Look for acknowledgment, a usable next step and

71
00:03:56.037 --> 00:03:59.748
an honest description of responsibility. Avoid an answer that

72
00:03:59.770 --> 00:04:03.148
dismisses the person, makes an unsupported promise or sends

73
00:04:03.170 --> 00:04:05.103
them elsewhere without explaining the route.

74
00:04:05.737 --> 00:04:09.226
Then draft a short progress statement for your own

75
00:04:09.248 --> 00:04:13.114
work example. Include the issue being addressed, the action

76
00:04:13.137 --> 00:04:16.381
taken or proposed, how its effect will be checked

77
00:04:16.670 --> 00:04:19.292
and the next review point. Keep the statement general

78
00:04:19.314 --> 00:04:21.470
and suitable for a learning exercise.

79
00:04:22.773 --> 00:04:25.440
You now have the main elements of a practical

80
00:04:25.462 --> 00:04:29.529
improvement: an understanding of the person's task, a barrier

81
00:04:29.551 --> 00:04:33.729
to investigate, an appropriate response, the roles needed to

82
00:04:33.751 --> 00:04:37.129
carry it out, input from people affected, evidence and

83
00:04:37.151 --> 00:04:38.907
a way to respond and report back.

84
00:04:39.428 --> 00:04:42.806
The final lesson will help you assemble those elements

85
00:04:42.828 --> 00:04:46.317
into one usable plan. It is not a test

86
00:04:46.339 --> 00:04:49.939
of memorizing all fifteen numbers. It is a chance

87
00:04:49.961 --> 00:04:53.139
to show how the standards support a thoughtful decision

88
00:04:53.161 --> 00:04:55.917
and a concrete next step in your work.
